Q: Does RavenDB (DabDB) support CROSS JOIN with the Northwind dataset? A: <p>The simplest and most efficient way is to load both collections separately on the client and compute the Cartesian product in code.</p>
<pre><code>using (var session = store.OpenSession())
{
    // Load all Orders
    List&lt;Order&gt; orders = session
        .Query&lt;Order&gt;()
        .ToList();

    // Load all Products
    List&lt;Product&gt; products = session
        .Query&lt;Product&gt;()
        .ToList();

    // Compute Cartesian product
    var cartesianProduct = from order in orders
                           from product in products
                           select new
                           {
                               OrderId = order.Id,
                               OrderDate = order.OrderedAt,
                               // Add any other Order fields...
                               ProductId = product.Id,
                               ProductName = product.Name,
                               // Add any other Product fields...
                           };

    // Now 'cartesianProduct' contains every Order-Product pair
    // You can iterate, paginate, or process it as needed
}
</code></pre>
<p>There are no reliable server-side options for a true Cartesian product. Attempts using multi-map-reduce indexes (e.g., fanout tricks or artificial grouping keys) often fail in edge cases (like empty collections) and are not intended for this purpose. They can also be inefficient or incomplete.</p>

Q: Diesel Enum Error: ChannelType Trait Bound Not Satisfied in Rust/Postgres A: <p>It looks like you're using the <a href="https://crates.io/crates/diesel-enum" rel="nofollow noreferrer">diesel-enum</a> crate which pretty clearly does not support custom types - only built-in integer and string types. If you can or want to adjust your Postgres schema to use these more generic types then you would be able use this crate.</p>
<p>If you want to use a custom Postgres type then you'll have to handle the mapping more manually instead. Diesel itself has an <a href="https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/tree/main/examples/postgres/custom_types/src" rel="nofollow noreferrer">example</a> (in <code>examples/postgres/custom_types/</code>) that shows how to use a custom enum.</p>
<pre><code>// schema.rs

pub mod sql_types {
    #[derive(diesel::sql_types::SqlType)]
    #[diesel(postgres_type(name = &quot;language&quot;))]
    pub struct Language;
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// model.rs

#[derive(Debug, AsExpression, FromSqlRow)]
#[diesel(sql_type = crate::schema::sql_types::Language)]
pub enum Language {
    En,
    Ru,
    De,
}

impl ToSql&lt;crate::schema::sql_types::Language, Pg&gt; for Language {
    fn to_sql&lt;'b&gt;(&amp;'b self, out: &amp;mut Output&lt;'b, '_, Pg&gt;) -&gt; serialize::Result {
        match *self {
            Language::En =&gt; out.write_all(b&quot;en&quot;)?,
            Language::Ru =&gt; out.write_all(b&quot;ru&quot;)?,
            Language::De =&gt; out.write_all(b&quot;de&quot;)?,
        }
        Ok(IsNull::No)
    }
}

impl FromSql&lt;crate::schema::sql_types::Language, Pg&gt; for Language {
    fn from_sql(bytes: PgValue) -&gt; deserialize::Result&lt;Self&gt; {
        match bytes.as_bytes() {
            b&quot;en&quot; =&gt; Ok(Language::En),
            b&quot;ru&quot; =&gt; Ok(Language::Ru),
            b&quot;de&quot; =&gt; Ok(Language::De),
            _ =&gt; Err(&quot;Unrecognized enum variant&quot;.into()),
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
